// Snap! — the element card set (step 4), all 118. The eighteen bench elements get the full set;
// the other hundred get the lighter form (In the world + one line + numbers; Overview and Shells generated). Register: reimagine.md §15 —
// state the rule first, then the case, count the electrons out loud, no story.
// over  = the Overview tab paragraph
// world = the In the world tab paragraph (where you meet it, what it is for)
// lines = italic one-liners, shown one per tab
// num   = boil (°C), density (g/cm³ for solids and liquids, g/L for gases at room conditions), found (year)
window.SNAP_CONTENT = {
  H: {
    over: 'Hydrogen is the simplest atom there is: one proton, one electron. Electrons sit in shells around the nucleus, and the first shell has room for two. Hydrogen has one, so it is one short. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, and hydrogen gets there by sharing its electron with another atom that shares one back. Two hydrogen atoms do exactly that with each other, which is why hydrogen gas is H₂ and not single atoms floating around.',
    world: 'Most of the hydrogen near you is locked into water and into the molecules of living things. Every sugar, fat and protein is a carbon frame with hydrogen atoms on the outside. On its own it is a gas lighter than anything else, which lifted the old airships and is the reason they burned. Stars run on it: the Sun turns hydrogen into helium and the leftover energy is sunlight.',
    lines: ['Three quarters of all the ordinary matter in the universe is hydrogen.', 'A hydrogen atom is about 99.95 percent empty space, and so is everything made of atoms.', 'Hydrogen is the only element whose common form has no neutron at all.'],
    num: { boil:-253, density:'0.09 g/L', found:1766 } },
  He: {
    over: 'Helium has two electrons. The first shell holds two, so helium’s only shell is full. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, and helium starts out that way. It has no reason to take an electron, give one, or share one, so it bonds with nothing. Not with other helium atoms, not with anything. That is why it is a gas of single atoms all the way down to almost absolute zero.',
    world: 'Helium fills party balloons and the blimps that replaced the hydrogen airships, because it lifts nearly as well and cannot burn. Its serious job is cold: liquid helium is the coldest liquid there is, and it keeps the magnets in MRI machines and particle accelerators cold enough to work. Most of the world’s helium comes out of natural gas wells, where it collected from radioactive rock over millions of years.',
    lines: ['Helium never freezes at ordinary pressure, no matter how cold it gets.', 'Helium was found in the Sun, by its color in sunlight, 27 years before anyone found it on Earth.', 'Once helium escapes into the air it drifts up and leaves the planet for good.'],
    num: { boil:-269, density:'0.18 g/L', found:1868 } },
  Li: {
    over: 'Lithium has three electrons. Electrons sit in shells around the nucleus. The first shell holds two and it is full. The second shell holds eight, and lithium has one electron in it. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, and for lithium the easiest way there is to give that one electron away, which leaves a full shell underneath. Losing an electron leaves lithium with a positive charge, written Li⁺. That single loose electron is the whole story of lithium.',
    world: 'That easily given electron is what a battery needs: a lithium battery works by sending lithium ions from one side to the other and back, and the electrons take the long way around through your phone. Lithium is a soft silver metal, so light it floats on oil, and it is mined from salt flats and hard rock. A lithium salt is also one of the oldest medicines for mood.',
    lines: ['Lithium is the lightest metal; a lump of it floats on oil.', 'Most of the lithium in the universe was made in the first few minutes after the Big Bang.', 'The battery in a phone holds about a gram of lithium.'],
    num: { boil:1342, density:'0.53 g/cm³', found:1817 } },
  Be: {
    over: 'Beryllium has four electrons: two in the first shell, which is full, and two in the second, which holds eight. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so beryllium’s way there is to give both outer electrons away and become Be²⁺. It holds them tighter than lithium holds its one, because the nucleus has one more proton pulling, so beryllium is less eager to react and a good deal harder.',
    world: 'Beryllium is a light, stiff, gray metal that is nearly transparent to X-rays, so it makes the windows on X-ray tubes. Mixed into copper it makes springs and tools that do not spark. Emerald and aquamarine are beryllium minerals. The dust is poisonous to breathe, so it is handled carefully and used only where nothing else will do.',
    lines: ['Beryllium is transparent to X-rays, so it makes the windows on X-ray tubes.', 'The mirrors of the James Webb Space Telescope are beryllium, coated in gold.', 'Emerald is beryllium, aluminium, silicon and oxygen, with a trace of chromium for the green.'],
    num: { boil:2469, density:'1.85 g/cm³', found:1798 } },
  B: {
    over: 'Boron has five electrons: two in the full first shell, three in the second, which holds eight. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full. Boron sits in an awkward spot: three is too many to give away easily and five short is too many to gain. So boron mostly shares, three bonds at a time, and it never quite gets to eight. That leaves it always a little hungry, which is why its compounds are unusual.',
    world: 'Boron makes glass tough: borosilicate glass is the kind that goes from freezer to oven without cracking. Borax cleans laundry, boric acid kills roaches, and boron fibers stiffen the frames of bicycles and aircraft. Plants need a trace of it to grow. Pure boron is a hard, dark, brittle solid that is not much use by itself.',
    lines: ['Boron makes borosilicate glass, the kind that shrugs off a hot oven.', 'Boron burns with a bright green flame, which is where green fireworks come from.', 'Boron is one of the few light elements not made in stars; cosmic rays make it by breaking heavier atoms apart.'],
    num: { boil:3927, density:'2.34 g/cm³', found:1808 } },
  C: {
    over: 'Carbon has six electrons: two in the full first shell, four in the second, which holds eight. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so carbon needs four more. Four is exactly half of eight, and that is the point: carbon is no better at giving four than at taking four, so it shares, four bonds at a time, in any direction. It bonds to itself as readily as to anything else, and there is no limit to how long the chains and rings can get.',
    world: 'Every living thing is built on carbon chains: sugars, fats, proteins, DNA. Coal, oil and natural gas are old carbon chains. Pure carbon comes as graphite, the soft black stuff in a pencil, and as diamond, the hardest natural material, and the only difference between them is how the atoms are bonded. Carbon dioxide in the air is one carbon holding two oxygens.',
    lines: ['Carbon bonds to itself without limit; every living thing is built on those chains.', 'A pencil line and a diamond are the same atoms, bonded differently.', 'About 18 percent of your body weight is carbon.'],
    num: { boil:4027, density:'2.27 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  N: {
    over: 'Nitrogen has seven electrons: two in the full first shell, five in the second, which holds eight. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so nitrogen wants three more, and it gets them by sharing three pairs. Two nitrogen atoms can do that with each other, sharing three pairs at once in a triple bond. That bond is one of the strongest in chemistry, which is why nitrogen gas hardly reacts with anything.',
    world: 'Four fifths of the air is nitrogen gas, and almost none of it does anything, because of that triple bond. Breaking it takes lightning, certain bacteria, or a factory at high pressure and temperature, and that factory process makes the fertilizer that grows about half the world’s food. Nitrogen is in every protein and in DNA. Liquid nitrogen is what freezes things fast in a lab or a kitchen.',
    lines: ['Four fifths of every breath is nitrogen, and almost none of it reacts with you.', 'Lightning makes a little fertilizer every time it strikes, by tearing nitrogen molecules apart.', 'Nitrogen in explosives works the other way: the atoms rush back into that stable triple bond and release the energy all at once.'],
    num: { boil:-196, density:'1.25 g/L', found:1772 } },
  O: {
    over: 'Oxygen has eight electrons: two in the full first shell, six in the second, which holds eight. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so oxygen wants two more. It gets them by sharing two pairs, or by taking two electrons outright from a metal and becoming O²⁻. Oxygen pulls on electrons harder than almost any other element, so when it bonds, it takes more than its share, and that pull is why burning gives off heat.',
    world: 'A fifth of the air is oxygen gas, O₂, two atoms sharing two pairs. Nearly half the weight of the Earth’s crust is oxygen locked into rock and sand, and most of the weight of water is oxygen too. Burning is just oxygen bonding fast to something. Rust is oxygen bonding slowly to iron. Every breath you take is to get oxygen to your cells, where it burns sugar slowly and gives you the energy.',
    lines: ['Nearly half the mass of the Earth’s crust is oxygen, locked into rock.', 'The oxygen in the air was put there by living things; the early Earth had almost none.', 'Liquid oxygen is pale blue and sticks to a magnet.'],
    num: { boil:-183, density:'1.43 g/L', found:1774 } },
  F: {
    over: 'Fluorine has nine electrons: two in the full first shell, seven in the second, which holds eight. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so fluorine wants exactly one more, and no element wants anything more badly. Its nucleus has nine protons pulling on a small shell, so it grabs an electron from almost anything it touches and becomes F⁻, or shares one pair and keeps most of it. Fluorine reacts with nearly every other element.',
    world: 'Fluorine gas is too fierce to keep around, so you meet fluorine already bonded. The fluoride in toothpaste and tap water hardens tooth enamel. Teflon is a carbon chain wrapped in fluorine atoms, and nothing sticks to it because fluorine holds its electrons so tightly that nothing can get a grip. Fluorine compounds also cool refrigerators and etch the glass in frosted bulbs.',
    lines: ['Fluorine pulls harder on electrons than any other element; it even burns glass.', 'Several chemists were injured or killed trying to isolate fluorine before Moissan managed it in 1886.', 'Teflon is slippery because fluorine will not share its electrons with anything that lands on it.'],
    num: { boil:-188, density:'1.70 g/L', found:1886 } },
  Ne: {
    over: 'Neon has ten electrons: two in the full first shell, eight in the second, which holds eight. Both shells are full. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, and neon already is, so it has no reason to take, give, or share an electron. It forms no compounds at all. Neon is a gas of single atoms that do nothing but bounce off each other.',
    world: 'Neon is a trace in the air, about one part in 65,000, and it is collected by cooling air until it turns to liquid and boiling the neon off. Run electricity through a tube of it and its electrons jump up a level and fall back, giving off a red-orange light. That is a real neon sign. The other colors of “neon” signs are other gases or colored glass.',
    lines: ['A neon sign glows red-orange because that is the color neon’s electrons give off.', 'Neon is the fifth most common element in the universe and one of the rarest in the Earth.', 'Liquid neon can absorb forty times more heat per liter than liquid helium, so it is a better coolant when it is cold enough.'],
    num: { boil:-246, density:'0.90 g/L', found:1898 } },
  Na: {
    over: 'Sodium has eleven electrons: two in the first shell, eight in the second, both full, and one in the third. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, and for sodium the easiest way there is to give that one electron away, leaving a full shell underneath. It becomes Na⁺. Sodium gives its electron up even more easily than lithium does, because the outer electron is one shell further from the nucleus and held more loosely.',
    world: 'Sodium is half of table salt: one sodium, one chlorine, the electron handed over. It is a soft silver metal that catches fire in water, so you never see it bare. In your body sodium ions carry the electrical signals along your nerves. Sodium street lights are the yellow-orange ones, and the yellow is the color sodium’s electrons give off.',
    lines: ['Sodium is soft enough to cut with a butter knife, and it catches fire in water.', 'Sodium’s yellow is the same yellow you see when salt water boils over onto a gas flame.', 'A teaspoon of salt holds about twenty billion trillion sodium ions.'],
    num: { boil:883, density:'0.97 g/cm³', found:1807 } },
  Mg: {
    over: 'Magnesium has twelve electrons: two in the first shell, eight in the second, both full, and two in the third. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so magnesium’s way there is to give both outer electrons away and become Mg²⁺. It does that readily, and when it reacts with oxygen it gives off a very hot, very white light.',
    world: 'Magnesium sits at the center of every chlorophyll molecule, the part of a plant that catches sunlight. It is a light, strong metal used in laptop cases, car wheels and aircraft parts. Milk of magnesia and Epsom salt are magnesium compounds. A magnesium fire is almost impossible to put out with water, which is why old camera flashes used it and why fire crews treat it with respect.',
    lines: ['Magnesium sits at the center of every chlorophyll molecule, catching sunlight.', 'Burning magnesium is bright enough to hurt your eyes and hot enough to burn under water.', 'Most magnesium is taken from seawater, where there is about a gram in every liter.'],
    num: { boil:1090, density:'1.74 g/cm³', found:1808 } },
  Al: {
    over: 'Aluminium has thirteen electrons: two in the first shell, eight in the second, both full, and three in the third. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so aluminium gives away all three outer electrons and becomes Al³⁺. Three is a lot to give, so aluminium holds them more tightly than sodium or magnesium hold theirs, and it reacts less violently.',
    world: 'Aluminium is the most common metal in the Earth’s crust, but it was rare and precious until 1886, because prying those three electrons back from its ore takes a great deal of electricity. Now it is cans, foil, window frames, airplanes and power lines. It does not rust away like iron because the first thin layer of oxide seals the surface and stops the rest. Rubies and sapphires are aluminium oxide.',
    lines: ['Aluminium was once dearer than gold; Napoleon III served his best guests on it.', 'Recycling a can takes about five percent of the energy it took to make the aluminium in the first place.', 'A ruby is aluminium oxide with a little chromium; a sapphire is the same with iron and titanium.'],
    num: { boil:2519, density:'2.70 g/cm³', found:1825 } },
  Si: {
    over: 'Silicon has fourteen electrons: two in the first shell, eight in the second, both full, and four in the third. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so silicon wants four more, and like carbon it gets them by sharing, four bonds at a time. It bonds to itself less eagerly than carbon does, and much more eagerly to oxygen, so most silicon on Earth is silicon and oxygen bonded into rock.',
    world: 'Silicon is the second most common element in the crust. Sand, quartz, granite, clay and glass are all silicon bonded to oxygen. Pure silicon, grown as a single flawless crystal and sliced into wafers, is the heart of every computer chip and solar cell, because you can tune exactly how well it conducts electricity by adding a trace of something else. Silicones, the flexible rubbers, are silicon chains with carbon groups attached.',
    lines: ['Silicon is the second most common element in the crust, and the heart of every chip.', 'A chip’s silicon must be pure to about one atom in a billion.', 'Glass is silicon dioxide that was melted and cooled too fast to form crystals.'],
    num: { boil:3265, density:'2.33 g/cm³', found:1824 } },
  P: {
    over: 'Phosphorus has fifteen electrons: two in the first shell, eight in the second, both full, and five in the third. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so phosphorus wants three more and gets them by sharing three pairs, the way nitrogen does. Its outer shell is a size bigger than nitrogen’s, so it can also stretch to five bonds, and most of the phosphorus in the world is doing exactly that, bonded to four oxygens.',
    world: 'Phosphorus and oxygen together are phosphate, and phosphate is the backbone of DNA, the energy currency of every cell, and the mineral of bones and teeth. Farms need it as fertilizer, and it is mined from ancient seabeds. Pure white phosphorus glows in the dark as it slowly burns in air and bursts into flame if it gets warm; the striking strip on a matchbox is its calmer red form.',
    lines: ['White phosphorus glows in the dark as it slowly burns in air.', 'The word phosphorus means light-bearer, from that glow.', 'Every time a cell uses energy, it breaks a phosphorus-oxygen bond.'],
    num: { boil:280, density:'1.82 g/cm³', found:1669 } },
  S: {
    over: 'Sulfur has sixteen electrons: two in the first shell, eight in the second, both full, and six in the third. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so sulfur wants two more, like oxygen. It gets them by sharing two pairs or by taking two electrons from a metal and becoming S²⁻. It pulls on electrons less hard than oxygen does, and its outer shell can stretch to six bonds, which oxygen’s cannot.',
    world: 'Sulfur is a yellow solid that is found pure near volcanoes and hot springs, and its compounds are the smell of rotten eggs, skunks and struck matches. It is in two of the amino acids that make proteins, and the bonds between sulfur atoms are what hold the shape of your hair. Most of the sulfur mined goes to make sulfuric acid, the most produced industrial chemical in the world.',
    lines: ['Sulfur was the brimstone of the old texts; it burns with a blue flame.', 'A perm curls hair by breaking the sulfur bonds and setting them again in a new place.', 'Rubber is soft until sulfur is cooked into it; that is what makes a tire hold its shape.'],
    num: { boil:445, density:'2.07 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  Cl: {
    over: 'Chlorine has seventeen electrons: two in the first shell, eight in the second, both full, and seven in the third. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full, so chlorine wants exactly one more. It takes it from a metal and becomes Cl⁻, or shares one pair. It wants that electron nearly as badly as fluorine does, and it is a good deal easier to handle, so chlorine is the one people actually use.',
    world: 'Chlorine is the other half of table salt, and salt is where nearly all of it comes from. A little chlorine keeps a swimming pool clean and makes tap water safe to drink; it kills germs by tearing electrons off their molecules. Bleach is a chlorine compound. PVC pipe is a carbon chain with chlorine on it. Your stomach acid is hydrogen and chlorine.',
    lines: ['A little chlorine keeps a swimming pool clean; a lot of it was a weapon in 1915.', 'Chlorine gas is yellow-green, and its name comes from the Greek word for that color.', 'Safe drinking water, treated with chlorine, has saved more lives than almost any other invention.'],
    num: { boil:-34, density:'3.2 g/L', found:1774 } },
  Ar: {
    over: 'Argon has eighteen electrons: two in the first shell, eight in the second, eight in the third. An atom is stable when its outer shell is full. The third shell can hold eighteen in all, but eight is the number that counts as full for bonding, and argon has it. So argon takes nothing, gives nothing, shares nothing, and forms no ordinary compounds. It is a gas of single atoms.',
    world: 'Argon is about one percent of the air, far more than any other noble gas, and it is cheap because it comes out of the same plants that separate oxygen from air. It fills ordinary light bulbs so the hot filament has nothing to react with, sits between the panes of a double-glazed window as insulation, and shields the metal from oxygen during welding.',
    lines: ['Argon is about one percent of the air, and it is in every light bulb, doing nothing.', 'The name means lazy, in Greek, because it refuses to react.', 'Most of the argon in the air came from potassium in rocks, decaying over billions of years.'],
    num: { boil:-186, density:'1.78 g/L', found:1894 } },
};

// The other hundred, in the lighter form the plan promised (§13 step 4): an In the world
// paragraph, one italic line, and the numbers. The Overview and Shells tabs for these are
// generated from elements.js at runtime. Boiling points marked ~ are estimates.
Object.assign(window.SNAP_CONTENT, {
  K:  { world:'Potassium is the other alkali metal in your body: potassium ions inside your cells and sodium ions outside them are what a nerve signal moves around. Bananas, potatoes and beans are full of it. It is also the K in fertilizer, and a soft silver metal that burns violet in water.', lines:['Potassium in bananas is very slightly radioactive; so are you.'], num:{ boil:759, density:'0.86 g/cm³', found:1807 } },
  Ca: { world:'Calcium is the metal in bones, teeth, chalk, marble, limestone and seashells, always bonded to something because it gives its two electrons away so readily. Calcium ions are the signal that makes a muscle contract. Cement is cooked limestone, and hard water is water with calcium in it.', lines:['Calcium is the metal in your bones, chalk, marble and seashells.'], num:{ boil:1484, density:'1.55 g/cm³', found:1808 } },
  Sc: { world:'Scandium is a light metal that is scattered thinly through many rocks and concentrated in almost none, so it is expensive. A little of it in aluminium makes a stronger, lighter alloy for bicycle frames and aircraft parts, and scandium lamps give a daylight-colored light for stadiums.', lines:['Scandium was predicted, and its properties guessed, before anyone found it.'], num:{ boil:2836, density:'2.99 g/cm³', found:1879 } },
  Ti: { world:'Titanium is as strong as steel at a little over half the weight, and it does not corrode, because its surface seals itself with a thin skin of oxide. That is why it is in jet engines, hip joints and dental implants; the body accepts it. Titanium dioxide is the bright white in paint, paper, toothpaste and sunscreen.', lines:['Titanium is as strong as steel at half the weight, and the body accepts it as its own.'], num:{ boil:3287, density:'4.51 g/cm³', found:1791 } },
  V:  { world:'Vanadium is added to steel in small amounts to make it tougher and harder; wrenches, drill bits and car springs often carry it. Its compounds come in a whole run of colors, one for each charge it can carry, which is why it was named for a Norse goddess of beauty.', lines:['A vanadium solution changes color four times as it is reduced: yellow, blue, green, violet.'], num:{ boil:3407, density:'6.11 g/cm³', found:1801 } },
  Cr: { world:'Chromium is the shine on a chrome bumper and the reason stainless steel does not rust: it forms a thin, hard oxide skin that heals itself. Its compounds are colors, from the red of a ruby and the green of an emerald to the yellow of old school buses.', lines:['Chromium gives rubies their red and emeralds their green.'], num:{ boil:2671, density:'7.15 g/cm³', found:1797 } },
  Mn: { world:'Manganese is in nearly all steel, where it pulls out the oxygen and sulfur that would make the metal brittle. The dark powder in an ordinary battery is manganese dioxide. Living things need a trace of it, and manganese is at the heart of the part of a plant that splits water to make oxygen.', lines:['The purple of a permanganate solution comes from manganese carrying seven positive charges.'], num:{ boil:2061, density:'7.21 g/cm³', found:1774 } },
  Fe: { world:'Iron is the most used metal on Earth and the core of the planet is mostly iron. Steel is iron with a little carbon in it. In your blood, an iron atom sits at the center of each hemoglobin molecule and holds the oxygen. Rust is iron bonding slowly to oxygen and water.', lines:['Iron is the last element a star can make by fusion; beyond it, fusion costs energy.'], num:{ boil:2861, density:'7.87 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  Co: { world:'Cobalt gives glass and pottery their deep blue and it is in the cathode of most rechargeable batteries. Alloys with cobalt keep their strength red-hot, so they are used in jet turbines. Vitamin B12 has a single cobalt atom at its center.', lines:['Cobalt blue has colored glass for at least three thousand years.'], num:{ boil:2927, density:'8.86 g/cm³', found:1735 } },
  Ni: { world:'Nickel does not tarnish, so it plates other metals and makes coins. Most of it goes into stainless steel, and the rest into batteries and heat-resistant alloys. The Earth’s core is iron and nickel, and so are most iron meteorites.', lines:['A five-cent nickel is three quarters copper.'], num:{ boil:2913, density:'8.91 g/cm³', found:1751 } },
  Cu: { world:'Copper carries electricity better than any metal but silver, so it is in every wire, motor and circuit board. It was the first metal people worked, and bronze is copper with tin in it. Copper turns green as it slowly reacts with the air, which is why old roofs and statues look the way they do.', lines:['The Statue of Liberty is copper, and its green is a skin of copper compounds about as thick as paper.'], num:{ boil:2562, density:'8.96 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  Zn: { world:'Zinc coats steel so it does not rust; that is what galvanized means. Brass is copper with zinc in it. Zinc is the case and the negative side of an ordinary battery, and your body needs a trace of it for hundreds of enzymes to work.', lines:['A penny is zinc with a thin copper coat.'], num:{ boil:907, density:'7.13 g/cm³', found:1746 } },
  Ga: { world:'Gallium is a soft silver metal that melts at 30 °C, so it melts in your hand. Bonded to arsenic or nitrogen it is a semiconductor: gallium nitride is the blue in blue and white LEDs, and gallium arsenide is in the fast chips of phones and satellites.', lines:['Gallium melts in your hand.'], num:{ boil:2204, density:'5.91 g/cm³', found:1875 } },
  Ge: { world:'Germanium was the first transistor material, before silicon took over. It is transparent to infrared light, so it makes the lenses of thermal cameras and night-vision gear, and a trace of it in the glass core of an optical fiber is what guides the light.', lines:['Germanium was named for Germany, twelve years after gallium was named for France.'], num:{ boil:2833, density:'5.32 g/cm³', found:1886 } },
  As: { world:'Arsenic is the classic poison, and it works by jamming the enzymes a cell uses to make energy. Bonded to gallium it is a useful semiconductor. It was in old green paints and wallpapers, and it is a natural contaminant of well water in many parts of the world.', lines:['Arsenic is in gallium arsenide, one of the fastest chip materials there is.'], num:{ boil:614, density:'5.78 g/cm³', found:1250 } },
  Se: { world:'Selenium conducts electricity better in light than in the dark, which made the first photocopiers and light meters work. Your body needs a trace of it, from nuts and fish, and too much is poisonous. A little selenium in glass takes out the green tint of iron.', lines:['Brazil nuts hold more selenium than any other common food.'], num:{ boil:685, density:'4.81 g/cm³', found:1817 } },
  Br: { world:'Bromine is a dark red liquid with a choking smell that stays in the bottle. Its compounds are flame retardants in plastics and furniture, and bromine salts in a hot tub do the job chlorine does in a pool. The old purple dye of kings came from a bromine compound in sea snails.', lines:['Bromine is one of only two elements that are liquid at room temperature.'], num:{ boil:59, density:'3.10 g/cm³', found:1826 } },
  Kr: { world:'Krypton is a rare gas in the air, about one part in a million. It fills some high-performance light bulbs and the bright flash tubes on airport runways. From 1960 to 1983 the meter was defined as a number of wavelengths of krypton’s orange-red light.', lines:['Krypton was the meter, for twenty-three years.'], num:{ boil:-153, density:'3.75 g/L', found:1898 } },
  Rb: { world:'Rubidium is a soft alkali metal that catches fire in air and explodes in water. Its useful job is time: rubidium atomic clocks are the small, cheap kind in cell towers and GPS satellites. It was found by the color of its light, two red lines, in a spectroscope.', lines:['Rubidium is named for the deep red lines it makes in a spectrum.'], num:{ boil:688, density:'1.53 g/cm³', found:1861 } },
  Sr: { world:'Strontium is the red in fireworks and emergency flares. It behaves like calcium, so the body puts it in bone, which is why the radioactive strontium from bomb tests was a worry. Strontium salts are in some toothpastes for sensitive teeth.', lines:['Strontium is the red in fireworks.'], num:{ boil:1382, density:'2.64 g/cm³', found:1790 } },
  Y:  { world:'Yttrium is in the red phosphor of old color televisions and in white LEDs, and yttrium-aluminium garnet is the crystal inside many lasers. Added to some ceramics it makes them tough enough for jet engine coatings. It was found in a quarry in Ytterby, Sweden, which named four elements.', lines:['Four elements are named for one Swedish village: yttrium, ytterbium, terbium and erbium.'], num:{ boil:3345, density:'4.47 g/cm³', found:1794 } },
  Zr: { world:'Zirconium does not absorb the neutrons in a nuclear reactor and does not corrode in hot water, so it is the metal that holds the fuel. Zirconium dioxide is the diamond stand-in cubic zirconia and the white of many dental crowns.', lines:['Cubic zirconia sparkles more than a diamond, and is worth almost nothing.'], num:{ boil:4409, density:'6.51 g/cm³', found:1789 } },
  Nb: { world:'Niobium makes steel stronger for pipelines and car frames with only a pinch. Cooled far enough it becomes a superconductor, and niobium alloy wire is what winds the magnets of MRI machines and particle accelerators. It also gives a rainbow of colors when anodized, so jewelers like it.', lines:['The magnets of the Large Hadron Collider are wound with niobium-titanium wire.'], num:{ boil:4744, density:'8.57 g/cm³', found:1801 } },
  Mo: { world:'Molybdenum goes into steel to make it hard and heat-resistant; tool steel, gun barrels and boiler plate carry it. Molybdenum disulfide is a slippery black solid used as a lubricant. Plants and animals need a trace of it; the enzyme that turns nitrogen into fertilizer inside a bacterium has molybdenum at its heart.', lines:['Molybdenum sits in the enzyme that lets bacteria pull nitrogen out of the air.'], num:{ boil:4639, density:'10.2 g/cm³', found:1781 } },
  Tc: { world:'Technetium has no stable form; every atom of it decays, so there is almost none on Earth and it was the first element made by people rather than found. A short-lived kind of it is the most used tracer in medical imaging: tens of millions of scans a year.', lines:['Technetium was the first element made by people rather than found.'], num:{ boil:4265, density:'11.5 g/cm³', found:1937 } },
  Ru: { world:'Ruthenium is a hard, rare platinum-family metal. A little of it hardens platinum and palladium for electrical contacts and fountain pen tips, and ruthenium compounds are catalysts and the coating on the anodes that make chlorine.', lines:['Ruthenium is named for Russia, where its ore was found.'], num:{ boil:4150, density:'12.4 g/cm³', found:1844 } },
  Rh: { world:'Rhodium is the rarest and, most years, the most expensive of the precious metals. Nearly all of it goes into the catalytic converters that clean car exhaust. It is also the bright, tarnish-proof plating on white gold jewelry.', lines:['Rhodium has sold for more than ten times the price of gold.'], num:{ boil:3695, density:'12.4 g/cm³', found:1803 } },
  Pd: { world:'Palladium is in catalytic converters, where it turns the worst of the exhaust into carbon dioxide and water. It soaks up hydrogen like a sponge, up to 900 times its own volume, and it is in the tiny capacitors of every phone.', lines:['A piece of palladium can hold 900 times its volume of hydrogen gas.'], num:{ boil:2963, density:'12.0 g/cm³', found:1803 } },
  Ag: { world:'Silver conducts heat and electricity better than any other metal, and it kills bacteria, which is why it is in wound dressings and some socks. It was money for most of history. Photographic film worked because silver salts turn dark in light.', lines:['Silver conducts heat and electricity better than any other metal.'], num:{ boil:2162, density:'10.5 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  Cd: { world:'Cadmium was the negative side of rechargeable nickel-cadmium batteries and the bright yellow, orange and red of artists’ paints. It is poisonous and builds up in the kidneys, so it is being phased out of most uses. It still controls the neutrons in some nuclear reactors.', lines:['Cadmium yellow is still the yellow many painters will not give up.'], num:{ boil:767, density:'8.69 g/cm³', found:1817 } },
  In: { world:'Indium tin oxide is a transparent conductor, and a thin layer of it is what makes a touchscreen sense your finger. Indium is soft enough to press into a seal between metal parts, and a little of it lowers the melting point of solder.', lines:['Every touchscreen has a film of indium on it thinner than a soap bubble.'], num:{ boil:2072, density:'7.31 g/cm³', found:1863 } },
  Sn: { world:'Tin coats the inside of a “tin” can, which is steel, so the food does not touch the iron. Bronze is copper with tin, and pewter and solder are mostly tin. Tin ore was so valuable in the ancient world that ships sailed to Britain for it.', lines:['Tin cries: bend a bar of it and it creaks as the crystals shear.'], num:{ boil:2602, density:'7.29 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  Sb: { world:'Antimony hardens the lead in car batteries and bullets, and antimony oxide is a flame retardant in plastics and fabrics. Its black sulfide was the eye makeup of ancient Egypt. The metal is brittle and not much use alone.', lines:['Kohl, the ancient eyeliner, was powdered antimony sulfide.'], num:{ boil:1587, density:'6.68 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  Te: { world:'Tellurium is rarer in the Earth’s crust than gold. A little of it makes steel and copper easier to machine, and cadmium telluride is one of the two main materials for thin-film solar panels. Breathe in a trace and your breath smells of garlic for weeks.', lines:['Tellurium is rarer than gold, and a whiff of it gives you garlic breath for weeks.'], num:{ boil:988, density:'6.23 g/cm³', found:1782 } },
  I:  { world:'Iodine is a dark gray solid that turns straight into violet vapor when warmed. Your thyroid needs it to make the hormone that sets your body’s pace, which is why a trace is added to table salt. Tincture of iodine was the standard antiseptic for a century.', lines:['Iodine goes straight from solid to violet vapor when warmed.'], num:{ boil:184, density:'4.93 g/cm³', found:1811 } },
  Xe: { world:'Xenon is a heavy, rare gas in the air. It gives the bright blue-white of xenon car headlights and camera flashes, and it is the fuel of the ion engines that push some spacecraft. Breathed with oxygen it is a general anesthetic, a very expensive one.', lines:['Xenon is a working anesthetic; it is only too expensive to use.'], num:{ boil:-108, density:'5.89 g/L', found:1898 } },
  Cs: { world:'Caesium is the softest metal and one of the most reactive; it explodes on contact with water. The second is defined by a vibration of the caesium atom, and caesium clocks are the ones GPS and the internet keep time by. It was the first element found with a spectroscope.', lines:['A caesium clock defines the second.'], num:{ boil:671, density:'1.87 g/cm³', found:1860 } },
  Ba: { world:'Barium is the green in fireworks. Barium sulfate is the chalky drink you swallow before an X-ray of the stomach, because it blocks X-rays and passes straight through the body. Barium compounds are also in drilling mud and the glass of old television tubes.', lines:['Barium is the green in fireworks, and the white drink before a stomach X-ray.'], num:{ boil:1897, density:'3.59 g/cm³', found:1808 } },
  La: { world:'Lanthanum is the first of the rare earths, a row of fifteen metals so alike that it took a century to sort them out. It is in the glass of camera lenses, in the nickel-metal-hydride batteries of hybrid cars, and in the lighter flints that spark when struck.', lines:['Lanthanum means “hidden”: it hid inside a cerium ore for decades.'], num:{ boil:3464, density:'6.15 g/cm³', found:1839 } },
  Ce: { world:'Cerium is the most common rare earth, about as common as copper. Cerium oxide polishes glass and is in the walls of self-cleaning ovens, and cerium in catalytic converters stores oxygen and releases it as needed. The flint in a lighter is mostly cerium.', lines:['The spark from a lighter is burning cerium.'], num:{ boil:3443, density:'6.77 g/cm³', found:1803 } },
  Pr: { world:'Praseodymium gives glass a yellow-green tint and, mixed with neodymium, makes the dark goggles of glass-blowers and welders. It is part of the strong magnets in wind turbines and headphones, and it colors cubic zirconia green.', lines:['Praseodymium means “green twin”; its salts are green.'], num:{ boil:3520, density:'6.77 g/cm³', found:1885 } },
  Nd: { world:'Neodymium makes the strongest permanent magnets there are, and they are in every hard drive, phone speaker, electric car motor and wind turbine. Neodymium glass is purple and is the working part of many high-power lasers.', lines:['Neodymium makes the strongest permanent magnets there are.'], num:{ boil:3074, density:'7.01 g/cm³', found:1885 } },
  Pm: { world:'Promethium has no stable form, so almost none exists in nature and it is made in reactors. It has been used in tiny long-life batteries for pacemakers and space probes and in luminous paint. It was named for the Titan who stole fire from the gods.', lines:['There is about half a kilogram of promethium in the whole Earth’s crust at any moment.'], num:{ boil:3000, density:'7.26 g/cm³', found:1945 } },
  Sm: { world:'Samarium-cobalt magnets were the first really strong permanent magnets and they keep working hot, so they are in jet engine sensors and guitar pickups. A radioactive form of samarium is a drug for the pain of bone cancer.', lines:['Samarium was named for a mine official, Colonel Samarsky, the first person an element was named after.'], num:{ boil:1794, density:'7.52 g/cm³', found:1879 } },
  Eu: { world:'Europium is the red in the phosphors of old television screens and in the anti-counterfeit ink of euro banknotes, which glows red under ultraviolet light. It is the most reactive of the rare earths and tarnishes quickly in air.', lines:['Euro notes glow red under ultraviolet light because of europium.'], num:{ boil:1529, density:'5.24 g/cm³', found:1901 } },
  Gd: { world:'Gadolinium is the contrast agent injected before many MRI scans: its atoms have seven unpaired electrons and light up the picture. It also absorbs neutrons better than any other element, so it is in reactor shielding, and it is magnetic below room temperature.', lines:['Gadolinium is a magnet in a cold room and not one in a warm room.'], num:{ boil:3273, density:'7.90 g/cm³', found:1880 } },
  Tb: { world:'Terbium is the green in the phosphors of fluorescent lamps and screens. An alloy of terbium, dysprosium and iron changes shape in a magnetic field, and that is used in sonar and in flat speakers that turn a whole surface into a loudspeaker.', lines:['Terbium alloy speakers can turn a window pane into a loudspeaker.'], num:{ boil:3230, density:'8.23 g/cm³', found:1843 } },
  Dy: { world:'Dysprosium is added to neodymium magnets so they keep their strength when hot, which matters inside an electric motor. Its name means “hard to get at,” and it still is: most of the world’s supply comes from a handful of mines.', lines:['Dysprosium means “hard to get at.”'], num:{ boil:2567, density:'8.55 g/cm³', found:1886 } },
  Ho: { world:'Holmium has the strongest magnetic pull of any element and is used to concentrate the field in the most powerful magnets. Holmium lasers cut and break up kidney stones. Named for Stockholm.', lines:['Holmium concentrates a magnetic field more than any other element.'], num:{ boil:2700, density:'8.80 g/cm³', found:1878 } },
  Er: { world:'Erbium is the amplifier in the optical fibers under the oceans: a trace of it in the glass boosts the light signal without turning it into electricity. Erbium lasers are used for skin resurfacing and dentistry, and erbium oxide colors glass pink.', lines:['Erbium in the fibers is why a signal can cross the Atlantic as light.'], num:{ boil:2868, density:'9.07 g/cm³', found:1843 } },
  Tm: { world:'Thulium is the rarest of the natural rare earths, about as rare as silver. A radioactive form of it makes a portable X-ray source that needs no electricity, and thulium is in some surgical lasers.', lines:['Thulium is named for Thule, the old name for the far north.'], num:{ boil:1950, density:'9.32 g/cm³', found:1879 } },
  Yb: { world:'Ytterbium is in some steels and in the fiber lasers that cut and weld metal in factories. Ytterbium atomic clocks are more accurate than the caesium clocks that define the second, and may replace them.', lines:['A ytterbium clock would lose about a second in the age of the universe.'], num:{ boil:1196, density:'6.90 g/cm³', found:1878 } },
  Lu: { world:'Lutetium is the last and hardest of the rare earths. Lutetium crystals are the detectors in PET scanners, and a radioactive form of it is a treatment for some cancers. Named for Paris, from its Roman name Lutetia.', lines:['Lutetium is the most expensive rare earth to isolate.'], num:{ boil:3402, density:'9.84 g/cm³', found:1907 } },
  Hf: { world:'Hafnium is chemically almost identical to zirconium, which is why it hid inside zirconium ores until 1923, but it absorbs neutrons strongly where zirconium does not. So hafnium makes reactor control rods, and zirconium makes the fuel cladding. Hafnium oxide is also in the transistors of modern chips.', lines:['Hafnium and zirconium are so alike they are the hardest pair of elements to separate.'], num:{ boil:4603, density:'13.3 g/cm³', found:1923 } },
  Ta: { world:'Tantalum is in the tiny capacitors inside every phone and laptop. It does not react with body fluids, so it makes surgical implants and bone screws. It resists acid so well it is used for the lab equipment that handles them.', lines:['Tantalum was named for Tantalus, who could not drink; it will not soak up acid.'], num:{ boil:5458, density:'16.7 g/cm³', found:1802 } },
  W:  { world:'Tungsten has the highest melting point of any metal, which is why it was the filament in every light bulb for a century. Tungsten carbide is nearly as hard as diamond and tips drill bits, saw blades and ballpoint pens. Tungsten is as dense as gold.', lines:['Tungsten has the highest melting point of any metal; that is why it is the filament.'], num:{ boil:5555, density:'19.3 g/cm³', found:1783 } },
  Re: { world:'Rhenium was the last stable element found, in 1925. It is one of the rarest metals in the crust, and most of it goes into the single-crystal alloys of jet engine turbine blades, which run hotter than the metal’s own melting point would allow without it.', lines:['Rhenium was the last stable element to be discovered.'], num:{ boil:5596, density:'21.0 g/cm³', found:1925 } },
  Os: { world:'Osmium is the densest element: a brick of it weighs as much as two bricks of lead. It is hard and brittle, and its main use is in tiny amounts to harden the tips of fountain pens and electrical contacts. Its oxide is poisonous and smells sharp, which is where its name comes from.', lines:['Osmium is the densest element; a brick of it weighs as much as two bricks of lead.'], num:{ boil:5012, density:'22.6 g/cm³', found:1803 } },
  Ir: { world:'Iridium is the most corrosion-resistant metal there is, and the standard kilogram was a platinum-iridium cylinder for more than a century. Spark plug tips and crucibles for growing crystals are iridium. A thin layer of it in rock around the world marks the asteroid that ended the dinosaurs.', lines:['A worldwide layer of iridium marks the asteroid that ended the dinosaurs.'], num:{ boil:4428, density:'22.6 g/cm³', found:1803 } },
  Pt: { world:'Platinum does not tarnish or react, so it is the metal of fine jewelry and the catalyst in car exhausts, oil refining and fertilizer plants. Platinum drugs are among the most used cancer treatments. The whole world produces less platinum in a year than it does gold in a month.', lines:['All the platinum ever mined would fit in an average living room.'], num:{ boil:3825, density:'21.5 g/cm³', found:1735 } },
  Au: { world:'Gold does not tarnish, it is soft enough to hammer into leaf a few atoms thick, and it has been money and treasure for as long as there have been people to value it. It carries electricity without corroding, so it plates the contacts in electronics. Gold is the most common metal in space suits: a thin layer on the visor reflects the sunlight.', lines:['All the gold ever mined would fit in a cube about twenty meters on a side.'], num:{ boil:2856, density:'19.3 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  Hg: { world:'Mercury is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature. It was the fluid in thermometers, barometers and old dental fillings, and the vapor in fluorescent lamps. It is poisonous and builds up in fish, so most of those uses are gone. Its symbol comes from its old name, quicksilver, in Latin.', lines:['Mercury is the other room-temperature liquid; iron floats on it.'], num:{ boil:357, density:'13.5 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  Tl: { world:'Thallium is soft, gray and poisonous; it was a rat poison until it poisoned too many people. Small amounts are in some glass and in infrared detectors, and a radioactive form is used to image the heart. Its name means “green twig,” for the color of its light.', lines:['Thallium was the poison in several famous murders before it was banned.'], num:{ boil:1473, density:'11.8 g/cm³', found:1861 } },
  Pb: { world:'Lead is soft, heavy and easy to melt, so the Romans made pipes of it, and it was in paint, gasoline and solder until its poison was understood. It is still the plates in a car battery and the shield around an X-ray room, because it blocks radiation well. Lead is where uranium ends up.', lines:['Lead is where uranium ends up: the last stop of its long decay chain.'], num:{ boil:1749, density:'11.3 g/cm³', found:'ancient' } },
  Bi: { world:'Bismuth is the heavy metal that is not poisonous, so it is in the pink stomach medicine and it replaces lead in shot, solder and fishing weights. It grows into stepped, rainbow-colored crystals when it cools. Bismuth is very faintly radioactive, with a half-life a billion times the age of the universe.', lines:['Bismuth crystals grow as rainbow staircases.'], num:{ boil:1564, density:'9.79 g/cm³', found:1753 } },
  Po: { world:'Polonium is intensely radioactive; a gram of it glows blue and heats itself to hundreds of degrees. It was the first element the Curies found, named for Marie Curie’s Poland. It has been used in anti-static brushes and as a heat source on spacecraft, and as a poison.', lines:['A speck of polonium is warm from its own decay and glows blue in the dark.'], num:{ boil:962, density:'9.20 g/cm³', found:1898 } },
  At: { world:'Astatine is the rarest natural element; less than a gram exists in the Earth’s crust at any moment, and no one has ever seen a visible piece of it because it would destroy itself with its own heat. It is being studied as a targeted cancer treatment.', lines:['There is less than a gram of astatine in the whole Earth’s crust at any moment.'], num:{ boil:'~337', found:1940 } },
  Rn: { world:'Radon is a radioactive gas that seeps out of the ground wherever there is a little uranium in the rock, and it is the second leading cause of lung cancer after smoking, which is why basements get tested for it. It is the heaviest gas there is.', lines:['Radon is the heaviest gas, nine times heavier than air.'], num:{ boil:-62, density:'9.73 g/L', found:1899 } },
  Fr: { world:'Francium is the last natural element to have been found, in 1939, and it is one of the two rarest: a few tens of grams in the whole crust at any time. Every atom decays within minutes. It has no uses; it exists to be studied.', lines:['Francium was the last element found in nature rather than made.'], num:{ boil:'~677', found:1939 } },
  Ra: { world:'Radium glows faintly on its own, which is what made the old watch dials and instrument panels shine, and what poisoned the women who painted them. The Curies took it from tons of ore a gram at a time. It was the radiation source for early cancer treatment.', lines:['Radium glows faintly on its own, which is what made the old watch dials shine.'], num:{ boil:1737, density:'5.5 g/cm³', found:1898 } },
  Ac: { world:'Actinium is the first of the actinides, the row of heavy radioactive metals that ends the natural table. It glows blue in the dark and is about 150 times more radioactive than radium. A short-lived form of it is being used to treat some cancers.', lines:['Actinium glows pale blue in the dark, from the air around it being ionized.'], num:{ boil:'~3200', density:'10.1 g/cm³', found:1899 } },
  Th: { world:'Thorium is about as common as lead and mildly radioactive, and it has a half-life three times the age of the universe. It was in gas lantern mantles and camera lenses. It could fuel a different kind of nuclear reactor, and several countries are working on that.', lines:['Thorium is named for Thor, and there is three times more of it than uranium.'], num:{ boil:4788, density:'11.7 g/cm³', found:1829 } },
  Pa: { world:'Protactinium is rare, radioactive, poisonous and expensive, and it has no uses outside research. It sits between thorium and uranium, and its name means “parent of actinium,” because that is what it decays into.', lines:['The world’s supply of pure protactinium is about 125 grams, made once in 1961.'], num:{ boil:4027, density:'15.4 g/cm³', found:1913 } },
  U:  { world:'Uranium is the heaviest element that occurs in any quantity in nature. It is dense, faintly radioactive, and about as common as tin. A rare form of it can split when a neutron hits it, and that is what powers nuclear reactors and the first atomic bombs. Uranium glass glows green under ultraviolet light.', lines:['Uranium was named for the planet found eight years before it.'], num:{ boil:4131, density:'19.1 g/cm³', found:1789 } },
  Np: { world:'Neptunium was the first element made beyond uranium, in 1940, and it is named for the next planet out. It shows up as a by-product inside every nuclear reactor. It has almost no uses, but it is one of the longest-lived wastes to store.', lines:['Neptunium is the planet after Uranus; plutonium is the one after that.'], num:{ boil:3902, density:'20.5 g/cm³', found:1940 } },
  Pu: { world:'Plutonium is made in reactors from uranium, and it is the fuel of most nuclear weapons and of the power packs that keep deep-space probes warm and running for decades. A lump of it is warm to the touch from its own decay. It is named for Pluto.', lines:['A lump of plutonium is warm to the touch from its own decay.'], num:{ boil:3228, density:'19.8 g/cm³', found:1940 } },
  Am: { world:'Americium is in most smoke detectors: a speck of it ionizes the air in a small chamber, smoke interrupts the current, and the alarm sounds. It is made in reactors and named for the Americas, matching europium above it.', lines:['Americium is in most smoke detectors.'], num:{ boil:2011, density:'12.0 g/cm³', found:1944 } },
  Cm: { world:'Curium is made in reactors and is intensely radioactive; it glows purple in the dark. Its heat has powered instruments on spacecraft, and the X-ray spectrometers on the Mars rovers use curium to make rocks give off their own X-rays.', lines:['The Mars rovers carry curium to make rocks glow in X-rays.'], num:{ boil:3110, density:'13.5 g/cm³', found:1944 } },
  Bk: { world:'Berkelium is named for Berkeley, California, where it and several of its neighbors were made. It has no uses; its only job has been as the target for making heavier elements, including tennessine.', lines:['Berkelium was the target that made tennessine, six elements down.'], num:{ boil:'~2627', density:'14.8 g/cm³', found:1949 } },
  Cf: { world:'Californium is a strong neutron source, and that is a real job: it starts up nuclear reactors, finds gold and silver in ore, and inspects welds and airline luggage. A gram of it costs tens of millions of dollars.', lines:['Californium sells for about 27 million dollars a gram.'], num:{ boil:'~1470', density:'15.1 g/cm³', found:1950 } },
  Es: { world:'Einsteinium was found in the fallout of the first hydrogen bomb test in 1952 and named for Einstein. It has been made in milligram amounts at most, and it destroys its own crystals with its radiation, so studying it is a race.', lines:['Einsteinium was first found in the debris of a hydrogen bomb.'], num:{ boil:'~996', density:'8.84 g/cm³', found:1952 } },
  Fm: { world:'Fermium was found in the same fallout as einsteinium and named for Enrico Fermi. It is the heaviest element that can be made in a reactor in weighable amounts; everything past it is made a few atoms at a time.', lines:['Fermium is the last element made in a reactor; the rest are made atom by atom.'], num:{ found:1952 } },
  Md: { world:'Mendelevium was the first element made one atom at a time, seventeen atoms in the first experiment, and it is named for Mendeleev, who built the periodic table. It has no uses and no stable form.', lines:['Mendelevium: seventeen atoms, the first time.'], num:{ found:1955 } },
  No: { world:'Nobelium is named for Alfred Nobel. Its discovery was claimed three times by three countries before the Russian claim was accepted. Its longest-lived form lasts under an hour.', lines:['Nobelium was announced three times before it was really made.'], num:{ found:1966 } },
  Lr: { world:'Lawrencium is named for Ernest Lawrence, who invented the cyclotron that made most of these elements possible. It closes the actinide row. Its longest-lived form lasts about eleven hours.', lines:['Lawrencium is the last actinide.'], num:{ found:1961 } },
  Rf: { world:'Rutherfordium is the first of the superheavy elements, made by firing one nucleus into another and catching the few atoms that stick. Named for Ernest Rutherford, who found the nucleus. It behaves like hafnium above it.', lines:['Rutherfordium is the first element past the actinides.'], num:{ found:1969 } },
  Db: { world:'Dubnium is named for Dubna, the Russian laboratory that made many of the heaviest elements. A few atoms at a time, each lasting about a day at most.', lines:['Dubnium is named for the town where it was made.'], num:{ found:1970 } },
  Sg: { world:'Seaborgium is named for Glenn Seaborg, who was alive at the time, the first person so honored. He had a hand in making ten elements.', lines:['Seaborgium was named for a living man, which had never been done.'], num:{ found:1974 } },
  Bh: { world:'Bohrium is named for Niels Bohr, whose model of electrons in shells is the one this bench uses. A few atoms at a time.', lines:['Bohrium is named for the man who put electrons in shells.'], num:{ found:1981 } },
  Hs: { world:'Hassium is named for the German state of Hesse, where the Darmstadt laboratory made it. It behaves like osmium above it, as far as anyone can tell from a handful of atoms.', lines:['Hassium is the heaviest element whose chemistry has been checked in an experiment.'], num:{ found:1984 } },
  Mt: { world:'Meitnerium is named for Lise Meitner, who explained nuclear fission and was passed over for the Nobel Prize. It has been made a few atoms at a time.', lines:['Meitnerium honors Lise Meitner, who explained fission and was denied the prize for it.'], num:{ found:1982 } },
  Ds: { world:'Darmstadtium is named for Darmstadt, Germany, home of the laboratory that made six elements. Its longest-lived form lasts about ten seconds.', lines:['Darmstadtium is the second element named for a city.'], num:{ found:1994 } },
  Rg: { world:'Roentgenium is named for Wilhelm Röntgen, who discovered X-rays. It sits under gold in the table, and would be a metal if anyone could ever make enough to see.', lines:['Roentgenium sits under gold, and no one has ever seen it.'], num:{ found:1994 } },
  Cn: { world:'Copernicium is named for Copernicus. It sits under mercury, and calculations suggest it might be a gas at room temperature, a metal that floats away. A few atoms have ever existed.', lines:['Copernicium may be the only metal that is a gas.'], num:{ found:1996 } },
  Nh: { world:'Nihonium is named for Japan, Nihon, the first element found in Asia. The Japanese team saw three atoms in nine years of running the experiment.', lines:['Nihonium: three atoms in nine years.'], num:{ found:2004 } },
  Fl: { world:'Flerovium is named for the Flerov Laboratory in Dubna. It sits under lead. Its longest-lived form lasts about two seconds.', lines:['Flerovium lasts about two seconds.'], num:{ found:1999 } },
  Mc: { world:'Moscovium is named for the Moscow region. It was made by firing calcium at americium, and it decays in under a second.', lines:['Moscovium was made by firing calcium into americium.'], num:{ found:2003 } },
  Lv: { world:'Livermorium is named for the Lawrence Livermore laboratory in California, which worked with Dubna on the heaviest elements. It sits under polonium.', lines:['Livermorium lasts about sixty milliseconds.'], num:{ found:2000 } },
  Ts: { world:'Tennessine is named for Tennessee, where the Oak Ridge laboratory made the berkelium target it was made from. It sits under the halogens but is too short-lived for anyone to know whether it acts like one.', lines:['Tennessine is the second-newest element, named in 2016.'], num:{ found:2010 } },
  Og: { world:'Oganesson is the heaviest element known and the only one named for a living person at the time, Yuri Oganessian. It sits under the noble gases, but calculations suggest it would be a solid and not noble at all. Five or six atoms have ever been made.', lines:['Oganesson: five or six atoms have ever been made, each gone in under a millisecond.'], num:{ found:2002 } },
});
